The issue is test isolation — the watch command from test_auto_check_appends_failure_output leaks into other tests because they run in parallel and share the global static. These async tests are setting/clearing global state concurrently. Let me fix these tests to be properly isolated — I'll use serial_test or just use unique watch commands and ensure each test is self-contained:

The real problem: tests run in parallel and one test sets the watch command that another test sees. The test_auto_check_silent_on_success sets true but sees the failing command from another test. And test_auto_check_truncates_long_output sees the wrong command too.

Since these share a global `WATCH_COMMANDS`, I need to either serialize them or make each test resilient. The simplest approach: wrap each test body so it sets the command immediately before executing and clears immediately after. But parallel execution means another test could set it in between.

The cleanest fix: use cargo test -- --test-threads=1 for these specific tests, or use a mutex. But the simplest approach is to make the tests not rely on ordering — just run them with #[serial] or restructure to not use the global.

